artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a soft amorphous material (e.g., a dense disordered packing of soft particles, an emulsion, a foam, or a metallic glass) subjected to a quasi-static or finite-rate simple shear deformation. Such a material exhibits a yielding transition: as the applied strain is increased, the macroscopic shear stress σ(γ) rises, reaches a peak, and—in the brittle regime—undergoes an abrupt macroscopic stress drop, often accompanied by the formation of one or more shear bands (narrow regions of strongly localized plastic strain). Address the following:

(a) In the brittle yielding regime, determine whether the formation of shear bands is the causal mechanism producing the abrupt macroscopic stress drop at yielding, or whether instead shear bands are a consequence of an independent underlying failure mechanism (e.g., a spinodal-like instability, a percolation of plastic events, or a fracture-like nucleation process), and provide a precise dynamical criterion distinguishing these two scenarios.

(b) Construct a quantitative continuum or mesoscopic theory of heterogeneous yielding flows that:
  (i) incorporates the effects of physical aging of the amorphous solid prior to and during deformation;
  (ii) accounts for inertial effects, including their interplay with aging;
  (iii) for ductile yielding, exhibits non-local rheological behavior characterized by finite-size effects and well-defined intrinsic spatial (cooperativity) length scales;
  (iv) respects the role of system size and boundary conditions (e.g., shear cells with rough vs. smooth walls, periodic vs. free boundaries);
  (v) yields qualitative predictions that can be tested and is in quantitative agreement with available experimental and numerical data on shear-band formation, stress drops, and spatial flow heterogeneity.

State necessary and sufficient conditions, in terms of material parameters (aging time, inertial number, cooperativity length, system size, boundary stiffness), under which the yielding transition occurs in the brittle (with macroscopic stress drop and persistent shear band) versus ductile (smooth, homogeneous) regime.

